    "q-39987": {
      "dom_id": "step-q39987",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "If $u=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2k \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]$ are orthogonal vectors, then $k=$",
      "worked_solution": "Since $u⊥v$, they are orthogonal vectors, we have that their dot product is equal to zero.\nFirst, let's calculate the dot product in terms of $k$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {v}_{4} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-5)+(-3)\\cdot 1+2k\\cdot 4+(-2)\\cdot k \\\\  & =-5-3+8k-2k \\\\  & =6k-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the dot product to zero, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6k-8 & =0 \\\\ 6k & =8 \\\\ k & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
