    "q-74190": {
      "dom_id": "step-q74190",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ -1 \\\\ b \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$ be vectors such that $u$ is orthogonal to $v$ and $w.$ What is the value of $a-b?$",
      "worked_solution": "Given that $u$ is orthogonal to $v$ and $w$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v=0 \\\\ u\\cdot w=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the first equation $u\\cdot v=0.$ Calculating the dot product in terms of $a$ and $b$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot 4+b\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot 5 & =0 \\\\ 2a-4-2b+10 & =0 \\\\ 2a-2b & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, for the second equation $u\\cdot w=0$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot w & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot (-3)+b\\cdot 1+2\\cdot (-3) & =0 \\\\ a+3+b-6 & =0 \\\\ a+b & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the values of $a$ and $b$, we need to solve the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2a-2b=-6 \\\\ a+b=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the second equation by $-2$ and adding the result to the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-4b=-12\\,⟹\\,b=3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $b=3$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a-2(3) & =-6 \\\\ 2a-6 & =-6 \\\\ 2a & =0 \\\\ a & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have that\n\n$$\na-b=0-3=-3.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
