    "q-140126": {
      "dom_id": "step-q140126",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$ be vectors such that $u\\,⊥\\,v.$ Find $(u-2v)\\cdot (v+2w).$",
      "worked_solution": "Since $u$ and $v$ are orthogonal vectors, we have that $u\\cdot v=0.$ Therefore, using linearity of the dot product to expand the parentheses, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u-2v)\\cdot (v+2w) & =u\\cdot v-2(v\\cdot v)+2(u\\cdot w)-4(v\\cdot w) \\\\  & =0-2(v\\cdot v)+2(u\\cdot w)-4(v\\cdot w) \\\\  & =-2(3\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot 2) \\\\  & +2((-2)\\cdot 0+5\\cdot 2+7\\cdot 0+5\\cdot (-3)) \\\\  & -4(3\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot 0+2\\cdot (-3)) \\\\  & =-2(21)+2(-5)-4(-2) \\\\  & =-42-10+8 \\\\  & =-44.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
